  "worked_solution": "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{\u2032} \\\\ (5)^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{5t})}^{\u2032} \\\\ {({e}^{4t})}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({t}^{2})}^{\u2032} \\\\ {(t-4)}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ t-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ t-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{2} \\\\ 4t-16\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{2}(t)$ only\".",
